"## Summary\n\nSecurity-ops follow-up 17: SECOPS-F16-01, the D-098 archive of run `1bfd5cff`, and one live AT29 run on agent-core 49.\n- **Session:** launched 01:03 EST on 2026-11-04 on #456's and #454's roadmap rows, and delivered at 02:00.\n- **Model:** codex `gpt-6-astra`.\n- **Size:** 3 commits and 183 owned files, +346,302/−6,308. Most of it is records: the D-098 archive of 127 root records and the new run's immutable `history/follow-up-17/` evidence.\n- **Checks:**\n  - merges cleanly onto current `main`; prepush-scan clean; owned-check passes. `scope-review.json` auto-merges with handover 43's digest changes.\n  - **The orchestrator reran these** on a scratch tree: current `main` (after #458) with isolation 22 and this branch merged in. All pass:\n    - `make -f mk/at-secops.mk at-secops-lint`, `at-secops-test` and `at-secops-build`, so the merged `scope-review.json` digests verify;\n    - `make -f mk/e2e.mk e2e-lint` and `go vet -tags e2e ./tests/e2e/...`;\n    - `go test ./tests/handover/...` and the committed-evidence tests;\n    - `make go-lint`, gofmt and `git diff --check`.\n- **Scope:** one live run in the lane's own namespace, with no rerun and no reservation. No product code, database edit or other lane's file changed. Zero owned Docker objects at the end.\n\n**The orchestrator's reading.**\n- **SECOPS-F16-01 was the setup's order, as the orchestrator read it.** The lane confirmed it from the product's code:\n  - a fleet cap change updates only the cap and its version. It changes no generation, and the cap is read only when a lease is issued (`internal/nodes/httpwire/fleet_config.go:54–67`, `internal/leases/leases.go:315–325`). The contract says it is not retroactive;\n  - a running node renews an ordinary granted lease only when its own remaining lifetime reaches the renewal lead, 120 s by default, with jitter (`node/agent/sync.go:85–166`, `engine.go:1547–1555`). Run `1bfd5cff`'s 86,400 s lease would have renewed the next day;\n  - **public routes that change a node's generation exist:** node PATCH, publication, grants, device admission and policy activation. So there is no product finding.\n- **The new setup: cap before admission.**\n  - The public fleet PUT and its read-back come before the case's existing finite grant and native device join. Their new generation makes the node ask for a fresh lease, issued under the 60 s cap.\n  - **The wait accepts only:**\n    - a lease the node has locally accepted;\n    - issued strictly after the cap's read-back, signed at 60 s, with a lifetime in (0, 60 s];\n    - a valid monotonic anchor and member coverage;\n    - a serving heartbeat whose policy deadline is D.\n    On a timeout it fails closed, naming the lease it saw. The post-disconnection read checks the lease again.\n  - **Unchanged:** follow-up 16's continuity evaluator and synthetic proofs, every other sub-case, and the public restoration of the cap, control, the worker and the member's standing.\n  - **Offline proofs:** run `1bfd5cff`'s evidence still fails, naming its 86,400 s lease. A post-cap 60 s lease passes. Pre-cap and equal-time short leases, overlong lifetimes, a missing member or anchor, too little remaining window and a wrong heartbeat all fail.\n- **The run:** `b2de46c8`, 06:29–06:42Z, on `d61a2db2`, which is `04d31ea5` plus this lane's setup. That base includes agent-core 49 (#454). One attempt, no rerun.\n  - **Confounded under D-133:** both pre-run inventories were empty after a 23-minute wait, but other lanes' suites appeared from 06:29:11Z, 13 samples of them during the control-loss case itself. No unconfounded claim is made.\n- **The control-loss case passed, so SECOPS-F16-01 is answered.**\n  - **Lease `bef669a3`:** issued 06:36:52.27Z, 13.1 s after the cap's read-back, with a signed cap and duration of 60 s.\n  - **Control loss** began at 06:36:59.78Z. The agent's own log shows its control requests ending, every 5 s. The worker was paused, and the agent kept running.\n  - **One agent identity** in all 67 samples, PID 107426, through the window and the recovery.\n  - **Both transports,** TCP and authenticated UDP, stayed continuous through D − 101 ms − 1 s. Their last replies were seen at D − 0.94 s, and the first quiet samples at D + 1.06 s. The D + 3 s bound and the ten-second quiet requirement passed.\n  - The heartbeat said `serving 1` up to D, then `serving 0` with `lease_expired`, then `serving` again after recovery.\n- **The other sub-cases:** grant expiry, device revoke, manual grant withdrawal and forced relay passed. Guest clock rollback stays blocked: it needs an independently clocked disposable guest, which is the operator's.\n- **AT29 stays failed: the old killed-agent lease-expiry case failed (SECOPS-F17-01).**\n  - Its cached lease expired at 06:35:21.40Z. Two 60 s admission holds inside the case delayed its fault until 06:36:22.06Z, 60.7 s after that deadline.\n  - Control was available in between, so the node renewed. The heartbeat's D was the new lease's, and both quiet samples were 20 to 30 s before it. Replies still flowed, so the unchanged cutoff predicate failed the row.\n  - **This is the harness's timing, not a product deadline violation.** No predicate was changed, and nothing was rerun.\n  - **Routed to the lane's next follow-up:** re-read the node's accepted lease immediately before the fault, and keep admission waits out of a timed sub-case.\n- **Node writes:** no conflicts. The capacity PATCH and the publication PUT each took one attempt, with HTTP 202.\n- **Agent restarts:** three, in the unchanged cases. One successor started with a `boot_unverified` baseline after the old expiry; the other two adopted the serving rules. No `baseline_unverified` followed by `GENERATION_MISMATCH`, so F48-01's signature did not recur.\n"
